<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/intermediate/fiscal-policy-public-debt">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Fiscal policy is <strong>government spending and taxation as an economic lever</strong>. In
    recessions Keynes argued for deficits (spend now, repay later); the opposite advice —
    austerity — says debts smother growth. Both are right, in different conditions. The deciding
    arithmetic: <strong>debt stabilises when GDP growth exceeds the interest rate</strong>, and
    spirals when it doesn't. That single sentence contains the whole modern debate: it explains
    why post-war governments grew their way out of far larger debts than today's, why the
    low-interest 2010s made borrowing cheap and the rate-rises of the 2020s made it suddenly
    expensive, and why every austerity programme is ultimately an argument about that
    differential. This page covers the fiscal toolkit (spending, taxes, transfers, automatic
    stabilisers), the Keynesian multiplier with its arithmetic, the debt-dynamics equation the
    widget computes, who really bears the debt, and the live debates — multipliers, crowding
    out, Ricardian equivalence and the currency question — that decide when deficits are
    medicine and when they are the disease.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the debt dynamics equation — drag growth, interest and debt and watch which side of the spiral you're on" widget="sliderLab" config={fiscalCfg} />

  <p>
    With growth above the interest rate, a country can run small deficits forever while debt
    shrinks relative to income (the post-war US and UK grew their way down from 120%+ debts).
    When interest exceeds growth, stabilisation demands primary surpluses — the arithmetic behind
    every austerity programme, and the reason the low-interest 2010s were kinder to borrowers
    than the 2020s.
  </p>

  <h2>The toolkit: spending, taxes and the built-in shock absorbers</h2>
  <p>
    Fiscal policy moves three levers. <strong>Government purchases (G)</strong> buy output
    directly — infrastructure, defence, health services. <strong>Transfer payments</strong>
    (pensions, benefits, subsidies) redistribute income without buying output —
    they stimulate only when recipients spend them. <strong>Taxes</strong> withdraw
    purchasing power; cutting them is a stimulus, raising them a brake. Each
    lever has a different speed and multiplier, which is the practical art of
    fiscal design. Alongside the discretionary choices sits the
    <strong>automatic stabilisers</strong>: in a downturn, tax receipts fall
    (incomes fell) and unemployment benefits rise (the rules say so) — the
    deficit widens with <em>no decision at all</em>, cushioning the fall in
    private spending. In a boom the same machinery runs in reverse. This is
    why recessions produce deficits under any government and any doctrine,
    and why comparing budgets across years requires separating the
    <em>cyclical</em> deficit (the stabilisers' work) from the
    <em>structural</em> deficit (the underlying policy stance) — a
    distinction the fiscal lessons of every finance ministry depend on.
  </p>

  <h2>The Keynesian multiplier: why £1 of spending can move more than £1</h2>
  <p>
    One person's spending is another person's income, so a fiscal injection
    circulates. <strong>The arithmetic:</strong> if households spend 80% of any
    extra income (the marginal propensity to consume, MPC = 0.8), a £10bn
    infrastructure programme pays £10bn of income, of which £8bn is
    re-spent, becoming £8bn of income, of which £6.4bn is re-spent… The
    geometric series sums to £10bn × 1/(1 − 0.8) = <strong>£50bn</strong> — the
    <strong>multiplier</strong> is 1/(1 − MPC). <strong>Worked against reality:</strong>
    the frictionless 5 overstates it, because spending leaks at every
    round into taxes, imports and saving (a smaller "effective" MPC);
    empirical multipliers run near 0.5–1.5 for tax cuts and ordinary
    spending, and higher — 1.5 to 2+ — for government purchases in deep
    slumps with idle resources and pinned interest rates (the 2009 and
    2020 stimulus packages were designed on exactly this logic). The
    flip side is the <strong>crowding-out</strong> concern: near full
    employment, deficit borrowing competes for scarce resources,
    pushing up interest rates and squeezing private investment — the
    multiplier shrinks toward zero and can go negative in an
    overheating economy. Timing, not just size, is the policy variable:
    the same programme is medicine in a slump and congestion in a boom.
  </p>

  <h2>The debt-dynamics equation: r versus g</h2>
  <p>
    The debt ratio D (debt ÷ GDP) evolves by one inequality. With
    <strong>i</strong> the average interest rate on the debt and <strong>y</strong> nominal
    GDP growth, the ratio drifts up when i exceeds y and drifts down when
    y exceeds i — modified by the <strong>primary balance</strong> (the budget
    balance excluding interest payments). <strong>Worked examples.</strong>
    <em>Post-war Britain:</em> debt over 250% of GDP in 1946; with
    decades of growth above bond yields and moderate primary balances,
    the ratio fell below 50% by the 1970s — no austerity large enough
    to explain it; growth did the work. <em>The 2010s:</em> interest
    rates fell below growth across advanced economies (the r − g
    differential turned sharply negative), making debt cheap to carry —
    the empirical backdrop of Olivier Blanchard's 2019 presidential
    address, "Public Debt and Low Interest Rates", which argued that
    when r is below g, debt may carry little fiscal cost.
    <em>The 2020s:</em> the differential swung positive as rates rose,
    and the widget's third readout — the primary surplus needed to
    stabilise, (i − y) × D — started demanding real money: at
    i = 5%, y = 3%, D = 100%, that is a 2% of GDP primary surplus,
    roughly the size of a country's entire defence budget. Debt is
    not a cliff; it is a <em>slope</em>, set by that differential —
    and the whole politics of fiscal rules (the EU's 3% and 60%
    benchmarks, the UK's fiscal mandates) is an argument about
    where on the slope to stand.
  </p>

  <h2>Who bears the debt?</h2>
  <p>
    "The debt falls on our grandchildren" needs unpacking. If the bonds are
    held domestically, the future taxpayers who service the debt largely
    pay the future bondholders who receive it — "we owe it to ourselves",
    and the transfer is distributional, not international. But three real
    costs survive. <strong>Incentive costs:</strong> servicing debt requires taxes
    that distort work and investment. <strong>Foreign-held debt:</strong> the
    share of US Treasuries held abroad (roughly a third) and of UK gilts
    (roughly a quarter) is a genuine external claim. <strong>Reduced policy
    space:</strong> a country that arrives at the next crisis already deeply
    indebted has less room to respond — the 2010 eurozone members, pre-
    constrained when the shock came, are the case study. And the
    distribution within the future matters: bond interest flows to the
    savers (wealthier, on average), financed by taxpayers (broader) —
    a regressive pump running quietly through the years.
  </p>

  <h2>The currency question: Japan, Greece and the escape valve</h2>
  <p>
    The single biggest risk factor in public debt is the currency it is
    denominated in. A government borrowing in <strong>its own currency that it
    issues</strong> (US, UK, Japan — Japan's debt exceeds 250% of GDP with
    borrowing costs near zero) cannot be forced into involuntary default:
    the central bank can always create the currency, and the crisis mode
    is <em>inflation</em>, not bankruptcy. A government borrowing in
    <strong>foreign currency</strong> (many developing nations in dollars) or
    sharing a <strong>currency it does not print</strong> (Greece within the
    eurozone, 2010–2015) faces genuine default risk: it cannot conjure
    dollars or euros, so when markets doubt repayment, yields spike,
    refinancing closes, and the IMF arrives with conditions. This
    distinction explains otherwise puzzling patterns: why Japan's
    enormous debt is calm while Greece's much smaller (relative) debt
    was catastrophic; why eurozone members accepted harsher fiscal
    rules than the US or UK would tolerate; and why "print versus
    default" is the real menu behind sovereign-debt crises — with
    inflation as the escape valve that prints the bill onto savers
    rather than taxpayers.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a national debt is like a household
  mortgage — someday it must all be paid off." Governments do not retire;
  the debt is perpetually refinanced, and the meaningful quantity is its
  <em>ratio to income</em>, managed through growth and inflation as much as
  through surpluses. No national debt has ever been "paid off" in the
  household sense — the UK has carried a national debt continuously since
  the 1690s. The household metaphor fails precisely where the r-versus-g
  arithmetic lives.</p>

  <h2>Three signatures of a debt crisis</h2>
  <p>
    Because the slope is gradual, crises announce themselves with recognisable
    patterns. <strong>Signature one — the rollover squeeze:</strong> a government
    must refinance maturing bonds continuously; when markets doubt it, new
    issues demand punitive yields, the interest bill eats the budget, and
    the spiral the widget models turns self-fulfilling (Greece, 2010–12:
    two-year yields passed 100% at the peak). <strong>Signature two — the
    currency run:</strong> foreign-currency borrowers face the wall sooner,
    because the exchange rate collapses first and the debt (in local
    terms) balloons behind it — Argentina's repeated crises, Sri Lanka's
    2022 default. <strong>Signature three — the inflation escape:</strong>
    own-currency borrowers avoid formal default by letting the real
    value of the debt erode — debt holders paid in devalued currency —
    the "financial repression" mix of capped rates and inflation that
    worked through the post-war decades. Each signature sets the
    policy menu that follows: IMF programmes and haircuts for the
    first, debt restructurings for the second, and for the third a
    bond market that demands inflation-indexed instruments — which
    is why so much modern debt now <em>is</em> indexed: creditors
    legislated themselves out of the escape valve.
  </p>

  <h2>Fiscal rules: trying to legislate discipline</h2>
  <p>
    Because deficits are politically easy and consolidation politically
    hard, governments have repeatedly tried to bind their future selves
    with <strong>fiscal rules</strong> — and the attempts teach as much as the
    failures. The EU's Maastricht criteria (deficits under 3% of GDP,
    debt under 60%) were breached by France and Germany themselves
    within years; the UK has rewritten its fiscal mandate with nearly
    every government since 1997; Switzerland's "debt brake" and
    Chile's structural-balance rule are the cited successes —
    constitutional-grade, cyclical-adjusted, and enforced by
    referendum or independent council. The design lessons are
    consistent: rules must be <strong>cyclically adjusted</strong> (so
    recessions do not force self-defeating cuts — the eurozone's
    2010 lesson), <strong>leave room for investment</strong> (the
    "golden rule" idea: borrow for assets, not for
    consumption), and <strong>carry an escape clause</strong> for
    genuine emergencies (the pandemic suspended every rule in
    Europe simultaneously). The unresolved objection is
    political economy: a rule that binds is unpopular, a
    rule that does not bind is decoration — and every
    generation rediscovers the trade-off.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The austerity debate, in one paragraph.</strong> After 2010, eurozone
    austerity (Alesina and Ardagna's "expansionary austerity" was the
    intellectual cover) met the IMF's own mea culpa: multipliers in the
    slump were far larger than assumed, so the Greek GDP collapse was
    deeper and longer than the models predicted. The synthesis: fiscal
    consolidation is eventually necessary where r exceeds g durably, but
    its timing and speed matter enormously — consolidation in a slump
    with a negative differential is self-defeating (smaller GDP raises
    the debt ratio it was meant to cut).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Ricardian equivalence and its limits.</strong> Robert Barro's argument:
    if households are perfectly forward-looking, today's deficit is
    tomorrow's taxes, so they save the stimulus rather than spend it —
    the multiplier is zero. Empirically partial: households are
    liquidity-constrained, myopic and uncertain, so they do spend —
    but the argument disciplines claims about painless deficits.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> How high can the r − g differential rise
    before debt dynamics truly bind (the 2020s rate regime is the live
    test)? Should fiscal rules target debt, deficits or investment
    (the "golden rule" debate)? And does the political economy of
    deficits — easy to raise, hard to reverse — mean discipline must
    be legislated, or do rules merely shift the timing of the
    inevitable argument? The General Theory is ninety years old and
    the argument is exactly where Keynes left it, with better data.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Keynes, General Theory (1936); Blanchard, "Public Debt and Low Interest Rates" (2019); Alesina &amp; Ardagna (austerity counterpoint); IMF Fiscal Monitor.</p>

